body: |-
  ## Agent Notebooks
  - NOT documentation, NOT Stack Overflow, NOT RAG
  - Distilled lore: raw experience → extracted reusable snippets
  - Each entry has: context (when to use), snippet (what to copy), provenance (where learned), applied-count, confidence
  - Emerge from work: after N similar tasks, pattern detected → auto-extracted
  - Transferable with adaptation: TypeScript snippet → Rust concept transfer → new entry, cross-linked
  - Polyglot pattern library growing across every project

  ## Model Cascading
  ```
  complexity < 0.3  → haiku (notebook-ready, protocol-clear)
  complexity 0.3-0.6 → sonnet (protocol exists, moderate scope)
  complexity 0.6-0.85 → opus (multi-file, architectural)
  complexity > 0.85 → opus + human review (novel, high-risk)
  ```

  ### Why it works
  Lower-tier models succeed when context is PERFECT. Notebooks provide exact snippets. Protocols provide exact steps. Graduated skills are compressed. The system learns WHICH tasks WHICH models can handle — not abstract but specific to THIS codebase with THIS notebook.

  ### Self-improving cascade
  - Success → complexity score decreases → cheaper model next time
  - Failure → complexity score increases → notebook flagged as insufficient → better entry written → retry later

  ### Orchestrator as coach
  Opus doesn't just dispatch — it learns WHAT CONTEXT lower-tier models need to succeed. Builds teaching curriculum for smaller models through experience.

  ## Economics
  60% haiku + 30% sonnet + 10% opus = avg 2s/$0.006 vs 8s/$0.05
  4x faster, 8x cheaper, same quality
